		{
			dependencies: [
				"438276B3F0F5F994"
				"0BCC4B5B144C4EFA"
			]
			description: [
				"&dLevel Emitters&r are your way to activate or deactivate things based off the volume of items/fluids stored &adirectly&r in your ae2 system."
				""
				"Here the use-case will focus on the &6Redstone Card&r and an &6Export Bus&r. But keep in mind this will work with other redstone or bus/io that can take a redstone card."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Level Emitters themselves do not consume channels &7(More information on channels in Tier 1 [The Heart and the Brain])&r."
				""
				"This allows them to be slapped &6basically anywhere&r in your network, opening up a lot of &aautomation&r capacity."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"For this usecase we have this setup where a &n&6level emitter&r&r is pointed &oat&r an &n&bexport bus&r&r."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/level_emitter.png width:185 height:100 align:center}"
				""
				"&7The purple block is a creative mode block, you can ignore that&r"
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Here we have a level emitter set to look at the amount of &acharcoal&r in the system. The amount of charcoal is set to &n100&r."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/level_emitter_no_hover.png width:185 height:70 align:center}"
				""
				"The behavior the level emitter is dependent on the setting toggled on the little &credstone torch&r to the left."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"The default mode is to emit a signal when the amount of the item in the system is &b≥(n)&r where &b(n)&r is the number you set the emitter to."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/level_emitter_above_or_equal.png width:185 height:70 align:center}"
				""
				"In this case this means the &dlevel emitter&r will send out a &credstone signal&r when &o100 &o&nor more&r&r charcoal is in the network."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Toggling the torch gives you inverted behavior where the signal will be sent when the amount of items is &b<(n)&r."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/level_emitter_below.png width:185 height:65 align:center}"
				""
				"It is important to note here that it is strictly only &o&6less than&r&r and not &o&eless than or equal to&r."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				""
				"Now for the actual interaction with the &bexport bus&r."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/export_redstone_card.png width:185 height:83 align:center}"
				""
				"For the export bus to respond to the &dlevel emitter&r it will need a redstone card installed."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				""
				"The &bexport bus&r itself has several &7(4)&r ways to respond to a redstone signal. The next few pages will go over them."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"First mode &6Always Active&r will &centirely ignore redstone&r. For this example this is entirely useless, but is the default mode."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/export_redstone_card_one.png width:185 height:84 align:center}"
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Second mode &6Active Without Signal&r will &cdisable&r the export bus whenever a signal is applied. &7And visa-versa it will &aenable&r &7the export bus on the lack of a signal."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/export_redstone_card_two.png width:185 height:84 align:center}"
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Third mode &6Active With Signal&r is the inverted behavior of the second mode where the export bus will be &aenabled&r only when a signal is applied and &cdisabled&r otherwise."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/export_redstone_card_three.png width:185 height:84 align:center}"
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Fourth mode &6Activate Once Per Pulse&r will trigger the export bus to run &e&nonly once&r per pulse of signal."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/export_redstone_card_four.png width:185 height:84 align:center}"
				""
				"It is important to note here that the amount of &bAcceleration Cards&r will change the actual volume of items sent during the pulse."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Minor note, the &dlevel emitter&r can also take &bfuzzy cards&r that work much in the same way as other devices mentioned prior."
			]
			id: "5453F57FB425DD00"
			rewards: [{
				id: "3609C6F14F20ACDE"
				item: "nyagibits_bytes:tier_one_strawberry"
				type: "item"
			}]
			subtitle: "All things in order. All things in place"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "1C32D7418CD7F182"
					item: "ae2:redstone_card"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "730888D8C47F5A08"
					item: "ae2:level_emitter"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Digital Redstone"
			x: 3.0d
			y: 8.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["06082D1481C77AC6"]
			description: [
				"&dComparators&r are your friends when it comes to &eturning off your machines&r when the buffer output is full."
				""
				"&lWhy is this important?&r "
				"Well a lot of setups do &cnot auto-stop&r since they generate &bitem entities in world&r and computers are &o&ephysically limited by the laws of the universe&r&r and thus can not deal with several &ctens of thousands of cobblestone on the floor&r, resulting in the server admins putting &otheir thumbs into your eye sockets&r."
				""
				"&nSimple setups on the next page.&r"
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"This simple setup needs &b1 comparator&r and &b1 analog lever&r."
				""
				"{image:compression:textures/quests/simple_comparator.png width:185 height:100 align:center}"
				""
				"Pictured here the comparator is set to &6&ncompare mode&r&r and, with a signal strength of 15 on the side, will &aonly output if the chest is full to the brim&r."
				""
				"Now if you'd like the machine to turn off with less items in the chest it is as simple as switching the comparator to &6&nsubtract mode&r and lowering the lever's output signal."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"See that was not so bad, now please, don't brick servers <3"
				""
				"&7Note: Iron Chests do not work with comparators sorry!"
			]
			icon: "minecraft:comparator"
			id: "438276B3F0F5F994"
			rewards: [{
